# Getting started with Kettlecorn

```elixir
Mix.install([{:kettlecorn, "~> 0.3.0"}])
```

## About this notebook

Kettlecorn runs untrusted Elixir and Erlang in a sandbox. Each VM runs OTP
and Elixir in its own launcher process, using a BEAM emulator compiled to
WebAssembly. This notebook covers evaluation, persistent state, errors,
isolation, and limits. The **host** is this Livebook runtime; the **guest**
is the OTP system running inside Wasmtime.

Requires Elixir 1.20 or later on x86_64 or ARM64 Linux or Apple silicon macOS.

Kettlecorn is experimental; bugs in it or its dependencies may compromise
isolation. Read [sandbox and resource limits](../guides/sandbox.md)
before using it with untrusted workloads.

## Install the runtime

The Hex package doesn't contain the runtime: the emulators and the guest's
OTP and Elixir. `Kettlecorn.Install.runtime/1` downloads the archive for
this library version from its GitHub release, checks its SHA-256, and
unpacks it into your user cache directory. Kettlecorn finds it there
automatically. The archive includes the native launcher for your platform
with an embedded Wasmtime engine.

```elixir
{:ok, runtime} = Kettlecorn.Install.runtime()
```

`mix kettlecorn.install` does the same from a Mix project. For proxy
settings or a local release archive, see
[runtime downloads](../guides/running-applications.md#runtime-downloads)
and [offline deployment](../guides/running-applications.md#offline-deployment).

## Run one evaluation

`Kettlecorn.run/2` starts a fresh VM, evaluates code, and stops it. Use this
when runs must not share state:

```elixir
{:ok, %{"value" => 120}} =
  Kettlecorn.run("Enum.reduce(1..5, &*/2)",
    max_fuel: 50_000_000_000,
    max_lifetime: 30_000
  )
```

## Reuse a VM

`max_fuel` limits total execution in Wasmtime fuel units (roughly WebAssembly
instructions), and `max_lifetime` limits elapsed time in milliseconds. Both
include startup and apply across all evaluations in a VM.

`Kettlecorn.start_link/1` waits for the guest to boot. The launcher compiles
the emulator on a cache miss. Every VM copies the guest filesystem and
boots OTP. See the [performance guide](../guides/performance.md) for startup
costs.

This VM has a ten-minute lifetime. Rerun this cell if it expires.

```elixir
{:ok, vm} =
  Kettlecorn.start_link(
    max_memory: 256 * 1024 * 1024,
    max_fuel: 1_000_000_000_000,
    max_lifetime: 600_000
  )
```

## Evaluate code

`Kettlecorn.eval/3` returns the formatted result, captured standard output,
and reductions charged, even when you set no reduction limit. For bounded
plain data, it also returns the value itself:

```elixir
{:ok,
 %{
   "value" => [{1, 1}, {2, 4}, {3, 9}],
   "inspected" => "[{1, 1}, {2, 4}, {3, 9}]",
   "output" => "hi\n",
   "reductions" => reductions
 }} = Kettlecorn.eval(vm, ~S|IO.puts("hi"); Enum.map(1..3, fn n -> {n, n * n} end)|)

true = reductions > 0
```

`"value"` is structured data; `"inspected"` is its formatted text. Writes to
stderr bypass `"output"` and go to the VM's console output. Retrieve console
stdout and stderr together with `Kettlecorn.take_output/1`, or configure an
`owner: pid` to receive events labeled `:stdout` and `:stderr`.

The VM runs OTP. Processes, ETS, regular expressions and
protocols all work, and state persists between evaluations:

```elixir
{:ok, _} =
  Kettlecorn.eval(vm, """
  defmodule Greeter do
    def hello(name), do: "Hello, \#{name}!"
  end
  """)

{:ok, %{"value" => "Hello, Ada!"}} = Kettlecorn.eval(vm, ~S|Greeter.hello("Ada")|)
```

Variables bound by evaluated code are local to that evaluation. The module
above remains loaded, but this variable does not:

```elixir
{:ok, %{"value" => 42}} = Kettlecorn.eval(vm, "answer = 42")
{:ok, %{"value" => false}} = Kettlecorn.eval(vm, "Map.has_key?(Map.new(binding()), :answer)")
```

Pass values into each evaluation with `:binding`:

```elixir
{:ok, %{"value" => 42}} = Kettlecorn.eval(vm, "x * y", binding: %{x: 6, y: 7})
```

An exception in evaluated code returns an error result. The VM keeps running:

```elixir
{:error, %{"kind" => "error", "message" => message}} = Kettlecorn.eval(vm, ~S|raise "boom"|)
IO.puts(message)
```

## Isolation

The guest sees a private copy of the runtime's file system. By default it
has no network, can't start OS processes, can't load native code, and
doesn't inherit the host's environment variables. Denied operations return
error tuples or raise exceptions; environment variables that weren't passed
to the guest return `nil`. The VM keeps running:

```elixir
{:ok, %{"value" => {:error, :enoent}}} =
  Kettlecorn.eval(vm, ~S<File.read("/etc/passwd")>)

{:ok, %{"value" => {:error, :eacces}}} =
  Kettlecorn.eval(vm, ~S<:gen_tcp.connect({1, 1, 1, 1}, 80, [], 2_000)>)

{:error, %{"kind" => "error"}} = Kettlecorn.eval(vm, ~S<System.cmd("ls", [])>)

{:ok, %{"value" => nil}} = Kettlecorn.eval(vm, ~S<System.get_env("PATH")>)
```

The host file is absent (`:enoent`), socket access is denied (`:eacces`),
starting a command raises, and the host's `PATH` is absent (`nil`).

Writes succeed, but only in the VM's copy, which is deleted when the VM
stops. Halting the guest stops its VM:

```elixir
{:ok, %{"value" => "notes"}} =
  Kettlecorn.eval(vm, ~S<File.write!("/tmp/notes", "notes"); File.read!("/tmp/notes")>)

{:error, {:vm_down, :normal}} =
  Kettlecorn.run("System.halt(0)", max_fuel: 50_000_000_000, max_lifetime: 30_000)
```

`network: true` grants sockets and `http: true` grants WASI HTTP to any
reachable destination. Set either grant to `[allow: rules]` to limit its
destinations. The [network notebook](network.livemd#compare-network-grants)
shows both grants and an allowlist.

## Limits

An evaluation that runs past its `:timeout` is killed. The evaluator also
traces its descendants and attempts to kill them. The VM survives:

```elixir
{:error, %{"kind" => "timeout"}} =
  Kettlecorn.eval(vm, "Stream.repeatedly(fn -> 1 end) |> Stream.run()", timeout: 500)

{:ok, %{"value" => :still_here}} = Kettlecorn.eval(vm, ":still_here")
```

Guest code can disable tracing, so evaluation cleanup is best effort.
Use VM limits to bound untrusted code.

Use `max_reductions` to limit the work in one evaluation. The reply reports
the reductions charged, and the VM can still run another evaluation.

```elixir
{:error, %{"kind" => "reductions", "reductions" => used}} =
  Kettlecorn.eval(vm, "Stream.repeatedly(fn -> 1 end) |> Stream.run()",
    max_reductions: 20_000
  )

true = used > 20_000
{:ok, %{"value" => :still_here}} = Kettlecorn.eval(vm, ":still_here")
```

The VM as a whole has limits too: `:max_memory` (bytes of WebAssembly
memory), `:max_lifetime` (wall-clock milliseconds) and `:max_fuel` (CPU, in
Wasmtime fuel units). These limits can stop a VM without affecting the
others. Here a separate, unlinked VM runs out of memory:

```elixir
{:ok, small} =
  Kettlecorn.start(
    max_memory: 128 * 1024 * 1024,
    max_fuel: 100_000_000_000,
    max_lifetime: 30_000
  )

{:error, {:vm_down, reason}} =
  Kettlecorn.eval(small, ~S|Enum.map(1..100, fn _ -> :binary.copy("x", 10_000_000) end)|,
    timeout: 30_000
  )

reason
```

Fuel counts execution since VM startup. Memory reports the bytes of linear
memory currently allocated. See
[measuring resource use](../guides/sandbox.md#measuring-resource-use)
for the remaining stats fields.

```elixir
{:ok, %{fuel_consumed: fuel, memory: memory}} = Kettlecorn.stats(vm)
true = fuel > 0 and memory > 0
```

## Stop the VM

Give mutually untrusted tenants separate VMs. Stop a VM when you have finished:

```elixir
Kettlecorn.stop(vm)
```

